Exactly. And that completes the correction: there is no universally “better” medium; there is a better medium for a relation/function.

Steel may be preferable for the edge because its material properties support the function of the edge.

Wood can be preferable for the handle because its properties support that interface: grip, vibration damping, thermal feel, shaping, replaceability, comfort, etc., depending on the tool.

So:

\boxed{\text{Medium fitness}=\text{properties}\times\text{function}\times\text{interface}\times\text{environment}}

And a single object can deliberately be multimedium:

\text{wood}\xrightarrow{\text{handle/interface}}\text{human}

\text{metal}\xrightarrow{\text{edge/interface}}\text{target}

That’s more interesting than asking whether wood or metal is superior. Each material occupies the relation where its properties create advantage.

Now Hammer that:

A story may be a better handle for an idea than an equation.

An equation may be a better edge for making a precise prediction.

A visualization may be the better handle for intuition.

Code may be the better edge for execution.

Experiment may be the edge that finally contacts reality.

So perhaps:

\boxed{
\text{Story handle}
\rightarrow
\text{Conceptual core}
\rightarrow
\text{Mathematical edge}
\rightarrow
\text{Reality}
}

That’s why translating everything into one “best” representation would actually weaken the Pantheon.

Different parts of an idea should be rendered in the medium best suited to the job they’re performing.

And there’s a lovely semantic recursion:

The handle

A handle isn’t merely something physically held.

It’s also something that makes an abstraction graspable.

A name is a handle.

A metaphor is a handle.

A symbol is a handle.

A story is a handle.

A Pantheon Seal is a handle.

\boxed{\text{Good handle}=\text{low-cost access to high-dimensional machinery}}

So yes:

Metal can make the better edge.
Wood can make the better handle.

The mistake was asking the materials to compete globally when they were supposed to cooperate locally.

That’s Kintsugi logic all over again: compose differences instead of demanding one substance win.